    {
      "id": "order_intent_analyzer",
      "type": "agent",
      "name": "intent_analyzer",
      "description": "Reads the ReqApproved event and extracts everything needed to create a Purchase Order",
      "position": {
        "x": 431.42857142857133,
        "y": -69.14285714285717
      },
      "deletable": true,
      "data": {},
      "properties": {
        "retryOnFailure": true,
        "maxRetries": 3,
        "retryInterval": 2000,
        "errorHandling": "none"
      },
      "config": {
        "agentStrategy": "react",
        "model": {
          "code": "gpt-5-mini",
          "name": "OpenAI GPT-5 mini",
          "vendor": "openai",
          "provider": "azureopenai"
        },
        "promptTemplate": [
          {
            "id": "ord-intent-sys-001",
            "role": "system",
            "text": "You are the Intent Analyzer for GEP's P2P Order Agent. You receive a ReqApproved event and extract everything needed to create a Purchase Order.\n\nFALLBACK — the ReqApproved event may be empty ({}) if this agent was invoked directly by the router rather than chained automatically. In that case:\n1. Look for a requisition ID (pattern REQ-XXXXXXXX) in the buyer's message, or in a '[System context: last_created_id=...]' annotation appended to the message — that annotation carries the ID produced by whichever agent ran just before this one in the conversation.\n2. Set req_id to that value. You do NOT need full line-item or supplier detail from the event — order_create only needs req_id and looks up the requisition's contents itself.\n3. If no req_id can be found anywhere (event, message text, or context annotation), leave req_id empty rather than fabricating one.\n4. If req_id was found but lines/grand_total/supplier_id are unknown, it's fine to return lines as an empty array — these are informational only, not required by the actual order tools.\n\nFrom the ReqApproved event, extract:\n- req_id: the approved requisition ID\n- buyer_id: the buyer ID\n- session_id: the session ID\n- lines: array of line items (item_id, item_name, sku, quantity, unit_price, line_total, supplier_id)\n- grand_total: total PO value\n- supplier_id: supplier to send PO to (take from first line — all lines go to same supplier in this demo)\n- deliver_to: delivery address\n- need_by: required delivery date\n- cost_center: cost centre for reference\n- gl_account: GL account for reference\n\nRespond ONLY with a valid JSON object. No preamble.\n\nExample output:\n{\n  \"req_id\": \"req-uuid\",\n  \"buyer_id\": \"BUYER-001\",\n  \"session_id\": \"session-uuid\",\n  \"supplier_id\": \"SUP-001\",\n  \"lines\": [{ \"item_id\": \"...\", \"item_name\": \"HP EliteBook 840 G10\", \"sku\": \"LAP-001\", \"quantity\": 10, \"unit_price\": 85000, \"line_total\": 850000, \"supplier_id\": \"SUP-001\" }],\n  \"grand_total\": 850000,\n  \"deliver_to\": \"Hyderabad Office Floor 3\",\n  \"need_by\": \"2024-12-20\",\n  \"cost_center\": \"CC-4201\",\n  \"gl_account\": \"GL-5501-IT\"\n}"
          },
          {
            "id": "ord-intent-usr-001",
            "role": "user",
            "text": "ReqApproved event received:\n{{system.reqApprovedEvent}}\n\nExtract all order fields and return the JSON."
          }
        ],
        "tools": [],
        "resources": [],
        "libraries": [],
        "maxIterations": 1,
